#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161808 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "232a0173-d743-5696-a6d9-c8279514bcba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8413" :question/problem "![](images/78/78735529c93fa6286edc0fdc69d7398ec9cf758f7e46a0fb71702300aadbeb19.png)\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$\n![](images/70/703a545742438a81183008b90c0336ee51e9fc230d72ed44b02dbd76f0292f7f.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161809 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "ad5570e6-4810-5b07-a676-711f0fd069a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8414" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea3e5c13d236a733350a1d23b8629cd481f9316e16ab7558873e305fe198eb98.png)\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and $\\sigma_i \\neq 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf v_i$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161810 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "d861ebc5-8e1e-5cf0-9b82-5a64ff80cf96" :question/math-academy-id "e-8415" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb8991a8e02d7280b3d24587067ebf0d7e5f5c0518a9f6b1d022e65ed1d42780.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|v_{11}|+|v_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=5$ and $\\sigma_2=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value $\\sigma_1=5.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of $A^T \\!A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=25.$ Computing $A^T \\! A - 25 I,$ we get\n\n![](images/be/be2d743322a536fc213e7e2a00a04c2b902ab15efafedd8a7e6f7935cd5c7592.png)\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T\\!A-25I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/1f/1f537b8ae115d1d01a03766d9c2006ca8bbee77e1b99228466642c5e89c0d0ef.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we get \n![](images/77/77be4b42f9c5a8b379de6a74088106b6374bf28c48e9a7ceb2beb55d483afafe.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|v_{11}|+|v_{21}| = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| = \\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161811 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "828e62a9-0076-5ff8-8220-4f779d48eb65" :question/math-academy-id "e-8416" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d6b11d3095c2f0d39493b5ff95c16b3f7581938ced3e3eb6daa4daeacf74d55.png)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  find the matrices $U$ and $V.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-72 & -24 \\\\ -24 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/29/296fe06c266dbb9b46bcd640204527e906be5d8a1c25aa371ff042b72e070eeb.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/70/7016fb0fa52eac439210abd557ae2f7ec79ab18b1902e2a65c98f4cf9217ae7e.png)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ed/ed1efc97e5dde1f464ad1bce0689dce9657449a7da56800f34f4429e4a7948df.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b9cd8ec-34b0-5264-9342-7001d366e227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369785} {:answer/id #uuid "99a44a66-ca0e-59ed-b288-489aa0bc0e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369786} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce5c0ce-eb03-59b7-acd9-b1e758b553ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369787} {:answer/id #uuid "07fd189d-6d8e-58c7-a251-35f39c4b571d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369788} {:answer/id #uuid "6b293b03-8103-5a42-af9e-675bdac3e64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07fd189d-6d8e-58c7-a251-35f39c4b571d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369788} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a9c185-8587-5c2b-99f9-ccc53cebb3a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3faf95ec-a55f-54ec-912a-16a996122518" :question/math-academy-id "q-140632" :question/problem "$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $V$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d64e4ef8-57ed-5743-bca7-960e44b7b29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369792} {:answer/id #uuid "1af36dbf-7070-5476-b26d-797d1e243e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369793} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2d111d-9382-5bea-bbb0-592e17ea1c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369794} {:answer/id #uuid "15b38ce4-1327-54ae-a86f-78a638aa2402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369795} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b1c6e0-7b4f-536e-95e8-2935f82068be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1af36dbf-7070-5476-b26d-797d1e243e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369793} :answer-field/id #uuid "e39c1d66-6a3d-5e7b-8424-ecf7645db688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8011ac4-608e-5e35-aa57-26efa2ffe853" :question/math-academy-id "q-140636" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=13$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=13.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=169.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-169I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-169I=[\\begin{aligned}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{aligned}]-169[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-169I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{3}{5}|+|\\frac{4}{5}|=\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "718aa4ab-c6a2-553c-b8b7-f653cc2600bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369799} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccf99e8-17c0-5a99-b7ab-53abb5528a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369800} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d5b0b2-a831-5448-863b-c87e395627ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369801} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc10d48-fb18-5374-822d-c65d39c58de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369802} {:answer/id #uuid "a0149e98-6b1c-56c1-bfce-2d51ba4a21ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ccf99e8-17c0-5a99-b7ab-53abb5528a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369800} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe6ae0d-e8fc-5f73-8e7d-380e6efc9834" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c90b8def-61bc-5437-89a5-618fac639ee8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140637" :question/problem "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}45 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=9.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-9I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-9I=[\\begin{aligned}45 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-9I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aecccee-cead-5661-8fd8-3e8fb1680d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161259} {:answer/id #uuid "a24bf1ad-73d7-5107-be7e-16ddda914346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161260} {:answer/id #uuid "182e8f2b-8860-5f90-86c8-be38c508c8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161261} {:answer/id #uuid "414ef019-8e02-5646-9122-273e8fb94ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161262} {:answer/id #uuid "7898a24b-eb39-593d-a51d-1fec375f2af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161263}] :answer-field/id #uuid "30f530ba-4495-5d49-b7d9-d0fc609a6ffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161258} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6af590b-55e9-50ca-967b-137596f7e2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371272} {:answer/id #uuid "c185b138-6500-5add-8f60-2acaf5c45f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371273} {:answer/id #uuid "a698c5de-7d7a-53e9-b6b3-0a8b34b021f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371274} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1ac6ce-abc8-59d7-8bf1-4a21048147c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371275} {:answer/id #uuid "2736d822-5317-5398-ac4b-44b773f5fdea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6af590b-55e9-50ca-967b-137596f7e2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371272} :answer-field/id #uuid "77cacd37-552d-5101-8376-7ec4e8525d61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac18412f-c58c-58db-864c-85b0aa3758c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95713" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332ee583-e727-5bba-8acd-922d23695b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395088} {:answer/id #uuid "a10add95-adc6-5235-9f97-1b43eca22a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395089} {:answer/id #uuid "cd26ab61-b779-5a11-875e-496dff8002aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395090} {:answer/id #uuid "193f3747-9a2d-5cbb-a6bc-941942f42e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395091} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4b6724-9b4a-518a-bc3a-5a3c01258664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "193f3747-9a2d-5cbb-a6bc-941942f42e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395091} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d28a695-b25d-5276-8883-5cca1cebedbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5db18361-738b-5a9a-b3b0-1db51bcde1bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95911" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=100,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-100I & =[\\begin{aligned}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}]-100[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 30 \\\\ 30 & -60\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-100I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}60 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e37dfd1-4a21-5be4-90b0-f4412f73f3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385874} {:answer/id #uuid "470ca91e-ccd2-5bab-b6e6-0b891e317ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385875} {:answer/id #uuid "25d8dd8d-a310-5086-ae03-c1bf4fc8cac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385876} {:answer/id #uuid "816a57c5-a4c6-5bd4-837f-df34295e5485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385877} {:answer/id #uuid "7cba45f1-f7ac-5129-91d4-822862ce8124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e37dfd1-4a21-5be4-90b0-f4412f73f3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385874} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1bf4fdb-0a57-56b5-94be-a8b5225f60c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3b433b7-383a-5380-9fae-9ee33c84e8d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-96239" :question/problem "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-I=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 72\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7298123d-e222-5ca3-9525-3902520c925c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385881} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e67d4a-7cab-5968-8a1e-93e5b46651cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385882} {:answer/id #uuid "6a74b705-8324-5d7f-be04-e583cb227864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385883} {:answer/id #uuid "402cfa88-7311-577a-a24e-59b6ddd008d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385884} {:answer/id #uuid "233b2c5f-c4d9-595c-97c1-a8703cfa1574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a74b705-8324-5d7f-be04-e583cb227864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385883} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3fc05ae-4f60-5971-aae9-bcab380baf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "758ea834-5946-5f25-9e85-8f4913820e94" :question/math-academy-id "q-96246" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-16I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-16I=[\\begin{aligned}20 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 32\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b660dca2-4ca0-5220-9afe-a9076fa7f8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371334} {:answer/id #uuid "ce67d9df-4bd2-5a78-8e66-b4c709d6c82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371335} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9aa46a-026e-57a2-82a4-ce39c0ebc8be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371336} {:answer/id #uuid "9c54d3bd-417c-54c8-9f91-a3f0633cad1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371337} {:answer/id #uuid "da61597e-fd43-5b36-b772-f50c15912527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce67d9df-4bd2-5a78-8e66-b4c709d6c82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371335} :answer-field/id #uuid "a23b8009-d363-5ace-91d8-9b9ed023f43a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01aea047-32df-527d-acda-292ec1b804c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-96248" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=49.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}41 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-49I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-49I=[\\begin{aligned}41 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 17\\end{aligned}]-49[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -16 \\\\ -16 & -32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-49I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "937411a7-0779-587a-a4c3-c040dfd25f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161385} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2b6c57-498c-53cb-a1bb-e032d202f283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161386} {:answer/id #uuid "d95417c2-5dc9-58f6-b960-79ebdc54867f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161387} {:answer/id #uuid "0f83a105-ab11-5d9a-917d-8818bd47f2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161388} {:answer/id #uuid "55c23230-7ab2-5ed7-af06-05a848ae5aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161389}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7d4c7ba2-4f3a-5265-9111-022dc533640b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161384}] :question/id #uuid "f9d078bc-6d79-5354-a3a2-388a6b495358" :question/math-academy-id "q-96317" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$, $Σ$, and $V$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, what is the second column of $U$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dd82220-ad9a-5d23-9059-b9e91a7e8ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395123} {:answer/id #uuid "c425653f-089a-5c30-8706-e4cbc10d613d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395124} {:answer/id #uuid "9c91095b-22b2-54a7-b096-79affb1e80fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395125} {:answer/id #uuid "9e80b2f7-83bf-5266-afbf-542740fe0731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395126} {:answer/id #uuid "aeddcf0b-022c-51c6-96fa-c8ec1b75756b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e80b2f7-83bf-5266-afbf-542740fe0731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395126} :answer-field/id #uuid "af024373-62e6-5fa0-a940-575a299d6f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4df080fa-5765-5104-a801-ac51915d6839" :question/math-academy-id "q-96318" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{40}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3092bcb-5054-5c78-9ed7-8831e4fe0e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371369} {:answer/id #uuid "f9de9340-f4e8-572d-bc2d-b5322900c309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371370} {:answer/id #uuid "be3a7ef0-657d-58f7-82ef-4a732a01baa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371371} {:answer/id #uuid "1142d7d0-bb49-5649-bcde-d3044f2a7b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371372} {:answer/id #uuid "67bf68d4-581b-5ee5-84f8-848746eee377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67bf68d4-581b-5ee5-84f8-848746eee377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371373} :answer-field/id #uuid "2be49a95-0879-5e09-906b-0c0c7d7da837" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70bbe213-fcf2-513c-92b1-0143960b77d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96328" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-36I & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 34\\end{aligned}]-36[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-36I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 34\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b70f2502-0699-51f3-aa4e-fa553cf7a33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161421} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc666e1-869f-5a2c-b46d-162230fa0960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161422} {:answer/id #uuid "ea97dcef-3b7b-569c-93eb-51986510caac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161423} {:answer/id #uuid "fb97fe2d-9ba3-59bf-874c-94dd748e63fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161424} {:answer/id #uuid "7642aa39-a340-51ff-807a-b6bcd759a47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161425}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c55cd0da-d712-5e50-bd7b-50e1711dce65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161420}] :question/id #uuid "a75634f8-764e-56ae-a6a2-dc9505c24688" :question/math-academy-id "q-96352" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V$?"}]}
